# Agent Boundaries & Delegation

Per-repository contract for the Next.js role agents installed in
`.opencode/agents/`. The orchestrator scopes each task with a written brief and
audits the resulting diff. Subagents carry **no tool-level permission blocks**:
they run their own commands, verify their own work, and are trusted to stay
inside the scope they were given.

---

## Roster

| Agent | Mode | Purpose | Writes |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `nextjs-orchestrator` | primary | Runs the pipeline, owns gates and the plan checklist | delegation, plan checklist, approved edge-test corrections |
| `nextjs-dev` | subagent | Components and pages | implementation, skeletons, dictionaries, barrel files |
| `nextjs-test-author` | subagent | Baseline and page suites | `*.test.ts(x)`, `tests/e2e/pages/**`, `test-plan.md` |
| `nextjs-qa-auditor` | subagent | Adversarial edge suites and audit | `*.edge.test.tsx`, `tests/e2e/components/**` |

Every agent works in its **own git worktree** on branch `agent/<slug>`; nothing
is written in the user's working tree. See
[`../skills/nextjs-dev-setup/references/agent-worktree-isolation.md`](../skills/nextjs-dev-setup/references/agent-worktree-isolation.md)
in the skills hub.

---

## Write Matrix

| Path | orchestrator | dev | test-author | qa-auditor |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `app/**`, `components/**`, `lib/**` | approve | write | deny | deny |
| `messages/**` | approve | write | deny | deny |
| `docs/design/**` | write | read | read | read |
| `docs/plan/05-pages-and-components.md` | write | deny | deny | deny |
| `*.test.ts(x)` | escalate | deny | write | deny |
| `*.edge.test.tsx` | user-approved only | deny | deny | write |
| `tests/e2e/pages/**` | escalate | deny | write | deny |
| `tests/e2e/components/**` | escalate | deny | deny | write |
| `test-plan.md` | approve | deny | write | read |
| `jest.config.*`, `playwright.config.*`, `package.json`, lockfiles | user only | deny | deny | deny |

`write` means the role owns the path. `approve` means the role coordinates the
change but does not perform it. `deny` means the role must report the need
instead of doing it — it is an instruction, not a tool lock.

---

## Enforcement

1. **Brief before spawn.** The orchestrator passes role, objective, mode,
   `worktree`, `tier`, `write_scope`, `read_scope`, `forbidden`, expected
   commands, and the definition of done. A subagent without a brief must return
   `BLOCKED: missing brief`.
2. **Self-verification, fast tier.** Subagents run their own linters, type checks,
   and targeted Jest files and return the real command output. They never wait for
   the user to approve a command, and never launch Playwright, full Jest, or a
   build during development.
3. **Audit on return.** The orchestrator runs `git status --short` and `git diff
   --stat` inside the worktree, compares the touched paths against `write_scope`,
   and restores anything outside it before accepting the work.
4. **No silent widening.** If the brief was wrong, the orchestrator re-briefs and
   logs the change; the subagent never expands its own scope.

---

## Rules

1. **One role per artifact.** No agent writes both implementation and tests.
2. **Edge tests are immutable.** Created once by `nextjs-qa-auditor`; only an
   explicit user decision changes one, and the orchestrator applies it and logs it.
3. **Baseline tests are mutable with a reason.** `nextjs-test-author` records
   every revision in `revision_reasons`.
4. **Failing test means failing code.** The default response is a
   `code_fixes_required` entry, not an assertion change.
5. **Only the orchestrator delegates.** The three role agents work directly and
   return their result; they never spawn each other.
6. **Subagents do not ask.** They return `BLOCKED` with the exact missing input
   instead of pausing the pipeline for a question.
7. **Writes go through the file tools.** Prefer `write`/`edit` over shell
   redirection so every change appears in `git diff` for the audit. Shell
   redirection is not forbidden, but it is not auditable and is treated as a
   boundary risk.
8. **The orchestrator audits the diff.** Before reporting success it verifies that
   each agent touched only its allowed classes of files, and re-runs the suite it
   claims is green.
9. **Fast tier during development.** Playwright, the full Jest project, `build`,
   and Storybook builds run at `git push` through `.husky/pre-push`. Authored E2E
   specs are reported `DEFERRED (pre-push)`, never `PASS`.
10. **One worktree per task.** Branch `agent/<slug>` off `main`, path
    `../<repo>-worktrees/<slug>`. Only the orchestrator (or a directly invoked
    `nextjs-dev`) merges it into `main`, with `--no-ff`.
11. **Conflicts belong to the user.** Abort the merge, keep the branch, report the
    conflicting paths, and ask which resolution to use. Never `-X ours`/`-X
    theirs`, never force-push, never rewrite history.
12. **No pushing.** Agents never `git push` and never use `--no-verify`.

---

## Repair Loop

```
fast suite fails
  -> owning test agent returns failures[] + code_fixes_required[]
  -> nextjs-dev fixes implementation (never the test)
  -> owning test agent re-runs the same files
  -> repeat until exit 0

pre-push fails (expensive tier)
  -> push is rejected by the hook
  -> reopen the loop on the same agent branch
  -> re-run the failing file with the real command, fix code, retry push
```

Escalation:

- Baseline wrong -> `nextjs-test-author` fixes it, logs the reason.
- Edge wrong -> user decision; orchestrator edits and logs it.
- Spec ambiguous -> `BLOCKED: ambiguous specification` with the rows in question.
- Merge conflict -> `BLOCKED: merge conflict` with paths; the user chooses.

---

## Worktree Lifecycle

```text
create  -> agent-worktree.ps1/.sh create <slug>   (branch agent/<slug>, base main)
work    -> implement + fast tests inside the worktree
commit  -> scoped conventional commit on agent/<slug>
verify  -> fast gates + write audit inside the worktree
merge   -> agent-worktree.ps1/.sh merge <slug>     (--no-ff into main)
conflict-> abort (exit 3), keep branch, ASK USER
cleanup -> agent-worktree.ps1/.sh remove <slug>     (after acceptance)
```

The pre-push hook lives in the shared `.git`, so it applies to every worktree.

---

## Harnesses Without a Roster

When `.opencode/agents/` is absent, run the same pipeline with prompt-level roles
and announce `DEGRADED: agent permissions unavailable`. The write audit is not
optional in that mode: it is the only boundary check.
